Setting. The page belongs to Grothendieck's 1983–84 notes on 'systèmes de pseudo-droites', i.e. arrangements of pseudolines in the real projective plane X. They are drawn in the usual disk model: the projective plane is a disk with antipodal boundary points identified. He also uses the double cover X~ (a sphere), as the tildes on this page and the 'fuseaux' (lunes) on the next page show.

(1) Top figures (orange arrangement with a green curve; boundary points 1..10, 11=1, plus a variant marked 10*, 6*). A distinguished pseudoline D (green) is moved through a fixed orange arrangement. Its successive 'stable' positions (general position with respect to the arrangement) are separated by elementary moves. In each move D crosses a vertex, i.e. a triangle flip or 'balayage de triangle'. Together these moves form a closed circuit D1, D2, ..., D9 = D1. The next page makes this explicit. The circuit depends on three choices:
- a 'sub-stable' starting position D passing through a vertex s;
- a 'rive' (side) ρ of D at s, i.e. which generic perturbation is taken first;
- an orientation ω of D, which fixes the order in which triangles are swept.
The inverse circuit is obtained by changing both ρ and ω. The numbering 1..10, 11=1 in the drawings records the boundary or crossing events along this tour. The starred variant (10*, 6*, with arrows near the endpoints) shows the effect of changing the starting choice at the 'infinite' end.

(2) Counting. The note reads 2n / 2(n+1) − 2 = 2n. Adjoining a line 0 to an n-line arrangement raises the number of boundary points on the line at infinity from 2n to 2(n+1). Discarding the two points belonging to the new line returns 2n. Equivalently, the circuit has 2n relevant steps.

(3) Boundary words. With the circle as line at infinity, an n-line arrangement gives the cyclic word 1 2 3 4 1' 2' 3' 4', where i' is the antipode of i. Inserting a new line 0 yields 0 2' 3' 4' 0' 1 2 3 4 1'. Brackets join i' to i; this is the half-period (antipodal) structure, i.e. the 'circular sequence' or allowable-sequence data.

The formula Circ(I∖{i0}) ← ĩ0 says the following. A lift or orientation ĩ0 of a pseudoline i0 determines a cyclic ('circular') order structure on the remaining index set I∖{i0}: the order in which i0 meets the other lines. In modern terms this is the local sequence of i0.

(4) Bottom-left disk and the formulas with u. Take i0 as the line at infinity. The n−1 other lines cut it in 2(n−1) points ξ0, ξ1, ..., ξ_{n−2}, ξ_{n−1} = ξ0', ξ1', ..., ξ_{n−2}'. Grothendieck encodes this as an orbit ξ_k = u^k ξ0 of a cyclic group of order 2(n−1), generated by u. The middle power u^{n−1} is the antipodal involution, which is why ξ_{n−1} = ξ0'. The rows 'α, u^n ξ0, u^{n+1} ξ0, ..., u^{2(n−1)−1} ξ0' list a half-period after inserting a new point α (or its antipode α'); compare the last disk, with α, α' and a rotation arrow. The rival row 'α, u^n ξ0, u^{n−1} ξ0, ..., u^{2n−2} ξ0' and the two versions 'ξ0 ξ1 … ξ_{n−1} ξ_n = ξ0'' versus 'ξ0 ξ1 … ξ_{n−2} ξ_{n−1} = ξ0'' show him fixing the index convention (n versus n−1 points).

(5) The diagram Δ~_{S~} →φ0 Δ~'_{S~0}, with φ1 : Δ~_{S~} → Δ~*_{S~1}. These are maps between objects attached to the double cover X~ and marked vertex data S~. They are the two arrangements or 'positions' obtained from a sub-stable position by its two generalizations (the two rives). This prepares the next page's torsor formalism: orientations Ω ≃ a ∧ Γ as contracted products of {±1}-torsors, and operations σ_i on 'repères' (frames) of X~.

Purpose. Grothendieck is building a combinatorial or groupoid description of the space of positions of one pseudoline relative to a given arrangement. Its pieces are:
- strata: stable versus sub-stable positions;
- edges: elementary flips;
- monodromy: the circuit around a vertex;
- the dependence of all this on orientation and side data, organized as torsors under {±1} and cyclic groups.
